(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["isha"]={"slug":"isha","name":"Īśā","deva":"ईशा","note":"","intro":["“That which is to be enveloped by the Lord”<br>All 18 Mantras — Śukla-Yajurveda, Vājasaneyī-Saṃhitā 40<br>Devanāgarī · IAST · Pada-by-Pada Gloss · Literal · Paraphrase · Five Commentaries","Sanskrit text courtesy of sanskritdocuments.org<br>(Mādhyandina recension: Vājasaneyī-Saṃhitā ch. 40; Kāṇva recension cross-checked).<br>English glosses cross-checked against Śaṅkara’s Bhāṣya (Gambhirananda tr., Advaita Ashrama),<br>Sri Aurobindo’s Īśa Upaniṣad (Pondicherry, 1924), Sivananda’s The Principal Upanishads,<br>Krishnananda’s Īśāvāsya Upaniṣad lectures, and Paul Deussen’s Sechzig Upaniṣad’s des Veda (1897)."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"1. About the Īśāvāsya Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Īśāvāsya — also called Īśopaniṣad or simply Īśā after its opening word — is the shortest of the ten principal Upaniṣads and the only one whose text forms the closing chapter (adhyāya 40) of a Saṃhitā rather than being detached as an Āraṇyaka or Brāhmaṇa appendix. It is the fortieth chapter of the Vājasaneyī-Saṃhitā of the Śukla-Yajurveda, attributed by tradition to the seer Yājñavalkya.","Because of its position, the Īśā is the only Upaniṣad that is itself part of the karma-kāṇḍa text, and the Indian tradition has read this as a deliberate structural statement: the karma-kāṇḍa ends, and exactly in the moment where ritual concludes, the Upaniṣad opens with ‘īśā vāsyam idaṃ sarvam’ — ‘all this is to be enveloped by the Lord.’ The text is therefore the bridge from ritual action (yajña) to non-dual knowledge (jñāna), and Śaṅkara opens his bhāṣya by saying precisely this.","In its 18 mantras the Īśā compresses almost every major theme of later Vedānta: (i) the world as Īśa-vāsya, woven through and enveloped by the Lord; (ii) action without grasping as a precondition of freedom; (iii) the suicide-by-ignorance of those who deny the Self; (iv) the paradox of the Ātman as simultaneously near and far, moving and motionless; (v) the equal cultivation of vidyā and avidyā, sambhūti and asambhūti, knowledge and action, the manifest and the unmanifest; (vi) the petition to Pūṣan to uncover the face of Truth; and (vii) the dying man’s prayer to be led upward by Agni.","Two recensions survive: the Mādhyandina (the standard Śukla-Yajurveda recension, the one Śaṅkara comments on) and the Kāṇva. They agree on 18 mantras and on substantially the same wording. The major recensional differences are in mantras 17–18 (the dying-man prayer), where the order, repetition pattern of ‘kṛato smara kṛtaṃ smara,’ and the precise reading of ‘vāyur anilam amṛtam’ vary. These are reproduced side-by-side in Part III below."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2. Manuscript Variations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Edition / Recension</td><td>Mantras</td><td>Veda</td><td>Notes</td></tr><tr><td>Mādhyandina (Vājasaneyī-Saṃhitā 40)</td><td>18</td><td>Śukla-Yajurveda</td><td>The most widely cited and commented recension. Basis of Śaṅkara’s Bhāṣya. The text used here.</td></tr><tr><td>Kāṇva</td><td>18</td><td>Śukla-Yajurveda</td><td>Identical text in mantras 1–16; mantra 17 differs in the placement of ‘oṃ krato smara’ and reads ‘vāyur anilam amṛtam’ as a single line. Mantra 18 substantially identical.</td></tr><tr><td>Sanskritdocuments.org</td><td>18</td><td>Śukla-Yajurveda</td><td>Mādhyandina recension; standard ITRANS encoding.</td></tr><tr><td>Paul Deussen (1897), Sechzig Upaniṣad’s des Veda</td><td>18</td><td>Śukla-Yajurveda</td><td>Translates the Mādhyandina; appends a long Einleitung treating the text as the earliest Upaniṣadic affirmation of karma-jñāna-samuccaya.</td></tr><tr><td>Sri Aurobindo (1924), Īsha Upanishad</td><td>18</td><td>Śukla-Yajurveda</td><td>Translates and comments on the Mādhyandina; reads the eight mantra-pairs as a single integrated argument culminating in mantras 17–18.</td></tr><tr><td>Hume (1921), The Thirteen Principal Upanishads</td><td>18</td><td>Śukla-Yajurveda</td><td>Standard early English translation; treats the order of vv. 9–14 as the original arrangement.</td></tr><tr><td>Olivelle (1998), Early Upaniṣads</td><td>18</td><td>Śukla-Yajurveda</td><td>Critical text; argues that the asambhūti/sambhūti verses (12–14) are an earlier stratum than the vidyā/avidyā verses (9–11), but preserves the received order.</td></tr></table>","All 18 mantras are present in every surviving recension. There is no major recension with fewer or more verses; the textual stability of the Īśā is remarkable, second only to that of the Māṇḍūkya."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"3. The Five Commentaries Summarised","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Because the Īśāvāsya does not have a single Sanskrit Brahma-Yogin commentary in the way the minor Upaniṣads do, the five reading-voices used in Part III are selected to span (i) the most rigorous classical Advaitin reading via its modern translator, (ii) two twentieth-century Vedānta voices, (iii) the leading European Indologist, and (iv) the leading modern integral-philosophical reader.","Swami Gambhirananda (Advaita Ashrama, Eight Upaniṣads with the Commentary of Śaṅkarācārya, vol. 1, 1957) — The standard English carrier of Śaṅkara’s Bhāṣya. Śaṅkara reads mantras 1–3 as addressed to the jñāna-niṣṭha (one fit for knowledge) and mantras 2–14 (with caveats) as accommodation language for the karma-niṣṭha. He famously holds that mantras 9–14 do not teach karma-jñāna-samuccaya but rather instruct the karma-niṣṭha to combine devata-jñāna (= avidyā) with karma (= asambhūti), so that he may purify himself to qualify for jñāna later. The ‘ātma-han’ of mantra 3 is the materialist denier of the Self.","Swami Sivananda (Divine Life Society, The Principal Upanishads, 1937; Īśāvāsya: Its Esoteric Meaning, lectures) — Practical sādhanā-Advaita. Frames the text as a manual for the householder-yogī: every mantra has a ‘what to do this morning’ corollary. Reads ‘tena tyaktena bhuñjīthāḥ’ (mantra 1) as the literal psychological move of renouncing-while-enjoying; treats mantras 6–7 as the inner-vision practice that grounds all karma-yoga; reads mantra 16 (‘so’ham asmi’) as a mahā-vākya equivalent.","Swami Krishnananda (Divine Life Society, The Philosophy of the Īśāvāsya Upaniṣad, 1971; lectures, Rishikesh) — Philosophical Advaita. Emphasises the structural integrity of the text: he reads the 18 mantras as three concentric circles — the cosmic (1–8), the epistemic (9–14), and the eschatological (15–18). His characteristic move is to argue against the Śaṅkara-school reading of vv. 9–14 as accommodation; for Krishnananda these verses really do teach an integrated path. He also reads the Pūṣan-prayer (15–18) as the moment of death of the karma-mūla.","Paul Deussen (1897, Sechzig Upaniṣad’s des Veda; Eng. tr. Bedekar/Palsule, Motilal, 1980) — Indological-historical reading. Notes that the Īśā is uniquely both Saṃhitā-text and Upaniṣad; treats mantras 17–18 as the oldest stratum, an actual funeral mantra that was secondarily incorporated; reads mantras 9–14 as a polemical insertion responding to early renouncer movements that devalued karma. His translation is consciously literal, often archaising; he is the source of most subsequent European translations.","Sri Aurobindo (1924; rev. 1965, Īsha Upanishad, Pondicherry) — Modern integral reading. Refuses both the karma-jñāna-samuccaya reading and the Śaṅkara-school accommodation reading: for Aurobindo the entire text is the single act of a single seer, and its argument is that the Brahman who is to be ‘worn’ (vāsya) by the world is the same Brahman who acts in it, so vidyā and avidyā, sambhūti and asambhūti, knowledge and works, are not stages but two sides of one realisation. The most metaphysically ambitious reading of the text; the only one that treats the Pūṣan-prayer as the climactic argument rather than a coda.","Under each mantra below the five voices appear in the following order: Gambhirananda (Śaṅkara) — Sivananda — Krishnananda — Deussen — Aurobindo."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Peace Invocation (Śānti-pāṭha)","title":"","sa":["ॐ पूर्णमदः पूर्णमिदं पूर्णात् पूर्णमुदच्यते ।","पूर्णस्य पूर्णमादाय पूर्णमेवावशिष्यते ॥","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["oṃ pūrṇam adaḥ pūrṇam idaṃ pūrṇāt pūrṇam udacyate |","pūrṇasya pūrṇam ādāya pūrṇam evāvaśiṣyate ||","oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"“Oṃ. That is the Full; this is the Full. From the Full the Full proceeds. Taking the Full from the Full, the Full alone remains. Oṃ. Peace, peace, peace.”","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["This is the canonical śānti-pāṭha for all Upaniṣads of the Śukla-Yajurveda (it also opens the Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka). It is mathematically remarkable: the Full minus the Full equals the Full. The arithmetic is impossible in any finite arithmetic, which is the point — the Pūrṇa here is not a quantity but the only thing there is, and any operation upon it leaves it untouched. Brahman is what remains when Brahman is taken away from Brahman. The Īśā opens, therefore, with a statement about the non-additive, non-subtractive nature of the One — which is what the eighteen verses then unfold."]}]},{"t":"","u":[{"ref":"The Eighteen Mantras","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Each mantra is given as: Devanāgarī → IAST → pada-by-pada gloss → literal translation → paraphrase → five-commentary digest.","Grammar abbreviations: m./f./n. = masc./fem./neut.; nom./acc./instr./dat./abl./gen./loc./voc. = case; sg./du./pl. = number; pres./impf./pf./pot./impv./ger./inf./caus. = tense/mood/non-finite; pp. = past passive participle; pap. = present active participle."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 1","title":"The Lord-clothed cosmos","sa":["ईशा वास्यमिदꣳ सर्वं यत्किञ्च जगत्यां जगत् ।","तेन त्यक्तेन भुञ्जीथा मा गृधः कस्यस्विद्धनम् ॥ १ ॥"],"iast":["īśā vāsyam idaṃ sarvaṃ yat kiñca jagatyāṃ jagat |","tena tyaktena bhuñjīthā mā gṛdhaḥ kasya svid dhanam || 1 ||"],"words":[["īśā","m. instr. sg. of īś","by the Lord"],["vāsyam","n. nom. sg. gerundive of √vas (to clothe)","to be enveloped / clothed"],["idam","pron. n. nom. sg.","this"],["sarvam","adj. n. nom. sg.","all"],["yat kiñca","indecl.","whatever"],["jagatyām","f. loc. sg. of jagatī","in the moving (world)"],["jagat","n. nom. sg.","the moving (thing)"],["tena","pron. n. instr. sg.","by that"],["tyaktena","n. instr. sg. pp. of √tyaj","renounced / abandoned"],["bhuñjīthāḥ","2 sg. pot. ātm. of √bhuj","you should enjoy / experience"],["mā","neg. (with injunctive)","do not"],["gṛdhaḥ","2 sg. inj. aor. of √gṛdh","covet"],["kasya svit","pron. m. gen. sg. + emph.","of anyone / whose"],["dhanam","n. acc. sg.","wealth"]],"lit":"“All this — whatever moving thing exists in the moving world — is to be enveloped by the Lord. By that-renounced, enjoy; do not covet anyone’s wealth.”","para":"All of moving existence is the dwelling-place of the Lord — wear that knowledge as a robe. Enjoy whatever life gives you, but through renunciation, not through grasping. Wealth never belonged to anyone — so what is there to covet?","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> vāsyam = ‘to be covered (with the awareness that this is the Lord)’. The addressee is the saṃnyāsin. tena tyaktena = ‘by that renunciation’ (instr. of cause), not ‘by that which is given up’: renunciation itself becomes the medium of enjoyment. mā gṛdhaḥ — because the wealth never belonged to anyone in the first place."],["Sivananda","Morning instruction: begin the day by mentally clothing every object you will encounter — the desk, the meal, the people — with Īśa-awareness. ‘Enjoy through renunciation’ is the householder’s yoga in a single line."],["Krishnananda","jagatyām jagat is a deliberate doubling — the-moving-thing in the moving-place — to underscore that ALL motion, without exception, is woven through with the unmoving Lord. The renunciation here is renunciation of ownership-claims, not of objects."],["Deussen","Notes that vāsyam from √vas (to clothe) was already glossed by some as from √vas (to dwell): ‘is the dwelling-place of the Lord.’ The gerundive sense favours the first; cites Atharvaveda 10.8 parallels on the cosmos as garment."],["Aurobindo","This is the single thesis of the entire Upaniṣad. The Lord-clothed cosmos is not a metaphor — it is what is. tena tyaktena bhuñjīthāḥ contains the whole programme: enjoy (bhuñjīthāḥ), but with renunciation as the medium. The later opposing pairs (vidyā/avidyā, sambhūti/asambhūti) are commentary on this single verse."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2","title":"Acting, one should desire to live a hundred years","sa":["कुर्वन्नेवेह कर्माणि जिजीविषेच्छतꣳ समाः ।","एवं त्वयि नान्यथेतोऽस्ति न कर्म लिप्यते नरे ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["kurvann eveha karmāṇi jijīviṣec chataṃ samāḥ |","evaṃ tvayi nānyatheto ’sti na karma lipyate nare || 2 ||"],"words":[["kurvan","m. nom. sg. pap. of √kṛ","performing"],["eva","indecl. emph.","only / just"],["iha","indecl.","here / in this world"],["karmāṇi","n. acc. pl.","actions"],["jijīviṣet","3 sg. pot. desid. of √jīv","should desire to live"],["śatam","num.","a hundred"],["samāḥ","f. acc. pl.","years"],["evam","adv.","thus"],["tvayi","pron. loc. sg.","in/for you"],["na anyathā itaḥ","neg. + adv. + adv.","not otherwise than this"],["asti","3 sg. pres. of √as","there is"],["na karma lipyate","neg. + n. nom. sg. + 3 sg. pres. pass.","action does not cling"],["nare","m. loc. sg. of nara","in a man"]],"lit":"“Performing actions only, here, one should desire to live a hundred years. Thus for you — and not otherwise than this — does action not cling to a person.”","para":"Live a full life of action, all hundred years of it. For a human being there is no other way. Done in this spirit (of īśā vāsyam), action does not stick.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> Addressed to the karma-niṣṭha (the one not yet ready for jñāna). For him there is no alternative path. This is not the highest teaching of the Upaniṣad — mantra 1 is. The two are stations, not equals."],["Sivananda","Legitimates the householder’s path. ‘Don’t run to the forest. Work, do your duty, but do it as offering.’ Quoted constantly in his writings on karma-yoga for laypeople."],["Krishnananda","Rejects Śaṅkara’s two-paths reading: the iha and the eva together insist that this is THE path, not A path. Karma in the spirit of mantra 1 IS the way; jñāna without karma is incoherent for an embodied being."],["Deussen","Parallels Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.4.22 (the jñāna-niṣṭha is told to ‘rise above’ sons and wealth). Reads the contrast between mantras 1 and 2 as the canonical proof-text for the karma-jñāna debate."],["Aurobindo","The hundred years is real — a full life, lived in the world, with all its works. The not-clinging arises not because the works are unreal but because the doer is no longer the ahaṃkāra. Karma done from the Self does not bind the Self."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3","title":"The Self-slayers go to demonic worlds","sa":["असुर्या नाम ते लोका अन्धेन तमसाऽऽवृताः ।","ताꣳस्ते प्रेत्याभिगच्छन्ति ये के चात्महनो जनाः ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["asuryā nāma te lokā andhena tamasāvṛtāḥ |","tāṃs te pretyābhigacchanti ye ke cātma-hano janāḥ || 3 ||"],"words":[["asuryāḥ","m. nom. pl. adj. of asura","demonic / asura-belonging"],["nāma","indecl.","by name / so-called"],["te","pron. m. nom. pl.","those"],["lokāḥ","m. nom. pl.","worlds"],["andhena","n. instr. sg.","(by) blind"],["tamasā","n. instr. sg.","by darkness"],["āvṛtāḥ","m. nom. pl. pp. of ā-vṛ","enveloped"],["tān","pron. m. acc. pl.","those"],["te","pron. m. nom. pl.","they"],["pretya","ger. of pra-i","having departed (after death)"],["abhi-gacchanti","3 pl. pres. of abhi-gam","go to / attain"],["ye ke ca","indecl.","whoever"],["ātma-hanaḥ","m. nom. pl. tatpuruṣa","Self-slayers"],["janāḥ","m. nom. pl.","people"]],"lit":"“Those worlds named ‘demonic,’ enveloped by blind darkness — to those, those people, whoever they are, who are Self-slayers, go after departing.”","para":"There are worlds called ‘demonic,’ shrouded in pitch darkness. To them go all who slay the Self — that is, all who deny it.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ātma-han = he who kills the Self, i.e., he who by ignorance fails to know the Self that is always there. The ‘killing’ is not ontological but epistemic: by not-knowing, you behave as if there were no Self, and thus go to worlds in which the Self does not appear."],["Sivananda","A stern warning verse. ‘The materialist who denies the soul has chosen his own hell.’ Sivananda often cited this verse in lectures to materialist visitors at the ashram."],["Krishnananda","The most striking pre-existential verse in the Upaniṣadic corpus — the suicide-by-misknowledge. The Self cannot be killed, but the relation to it can be severed; that severance is what is meant by ātma-han."],["Deussen","Parallels Kaṭha 1.2.6 and Maitri 4.2. The earliest occurrence of ātma-han as a technical term for the unreflective materialist. The ‘asurya lokā’ prefigures the Purāṇic naraka-loka but is here existential rather than cosmological."],["Aurobindo","The worlds of darkness are real-time experiential states, not posthumous places. The Self-slayer enters them already, in this life, by the act of denying the Self that he is."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4","title":"Unmoving, one, swifter than mind","sa":["अनेजदेकं मनसो जवीयो नैनद्देवा आप्नुवन्पूर्वमर्षत् ।","तद्धावतोऽन्यानत्येति तिष्ठत्तस्मिन्नपो मातरिश्वा दधाति ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["anejad ekaṃ manaso javīyo nainad devā āpnuvan pūrvam arṣat |","tad dhāvato ’nyān atyeti tiṣṭhat tasminn apo mātariśvā dadhāti || 4 ||"],"words":[["an-ejat","n. nom. sg. pap. of ej + neg.","unmoving"],["ekam","num. n. nom. sg.","one"],["manasaḥ","n. abl. sg. (compar.)","than the mind"],["javīyaḥ","n. nom. sg. compar.","swifter"],["na enad","neg. + pron. n. acc. sg.","this not"],["devāḥ","m. nom. pl.","the gods / senses"],["āpnuvan","3 pl. impf. of √āp","reached"],["pūrvam","adv.","already / first"],["arṣat","n. nom. sg. pap. of √ṛṣ","running ahead"],["tat","pron. n. nom. sg.","that"],["dhāvataḥ","m. acc. pl. pap. of √dhāv","(the) running ones"],["anyān","m. acc. pl.","others"],["ati-eti","3 sg. pres. of ati-i","surpasses"],["tiṣṭhat","n. nom. sg. pap. of √sthā","standing (still)"],["tasmin","pron. n. loc. sg.","in it"],["apaḥ","f. acc. pl.","waters / activities"],["mātariśvā","m. nom. sg.","Wind (he-who-moves-in-the-mother)"],["dadhāti","3 sg. pres. of √dhā","places / deposits"]],"lit":"“Unmoving, one, swifter than the mind — the gods did not reach it, going ahead. That, standing still, surpasses others as they run. In it Mātariśvan (Wind) deposits the waters.”","para":"The Ātman is one and unmoving, yet quicker than thought. The senses, running, never catch it. Standing still, it outpaces all that runs. In it the wind (cosmic dynamism) deposits all activities.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The apparent contradictions — unmoving yet swifter than mind, standing yet surpassing runners — must be read as via negativa. The Ātman has no motion of its own (it is unmoving), but every motion is its appearance (so it ‘outruns’ all). The ‘gods’ are the senses."],["Sivananda","Read this verse as evidence that the Self is closer to you than your own thought; meditation does not ‘reach’ it because it is already what you are."],["Krishnananda","The verse is constructed in tetra-paradoxes; each clause refutes both motion-language and rest-language. Motion and rest are categories of the world of forms; the Ātman, being neither form nor formless in any reducible sense, slips between them."],["Deussen","Cites Kaṭha 1.2.21 (‘āsīno dūraṃ vrajati’) as a near-parallel. Mātariśvan is here a cosmic principle (= prāṇa), not literal wind. Notes the term as archaic Ṛgvedic vocabulary preserved in this single Upaniṣadic verse."],["Aurobindo","The One that does not move outpaces everything moving because it is the substrate of motion. Reads apo dadhāti as: cosmic Energy (Mātariśvan) deposits the working-fluids of existence (apaḥ as kriyāḥ) in the silent Ātman."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 5","title":"That moves, that does not move","sa":["तदेजति तन्नैजति तद्दूरे तद्वन्तिके ।","तदन्तरस्य सर्वस्य तदु सर्वस्यास्य बाह्यतः ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["tad ejati tan naijati tad dūre tad v antike |","tad antar asya sarvasya tad u sarvasyāsya bāhyataḥ || 5 ||"],"words":[["tat","pron. n. nom. sg.","that"],["ejati","3 sg. pres. of √ej","moves"],["na ejati","neg. + 3 sg. pres.","does not move"],["tad dūre","pron. + n. loc. sg.","that is far"],["tad u antike","pron. + emph. + n. loc. sg.","that is also near"],["antar","adv./prep.","within"],["asya sarvasya","pron. + adj. n. gen. sg.","of all this"],["u","indecl. emph.","also / indeed"],["bāhyataḥ","adv.","outside (of)"]],"lit":"“That moves, that does not move; that is far, that is near. That is within all this; that also is outside of all this.”","para":"The Ātman both moves and does not move. It is at once far and near. It is within all that is, and it is outside all that is. There is no place where it is and no place where it is not.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The same Ātman appears as moving (with respect to the limiting adjuncts) and as unmoving (in itself). ‘Far’ to the ignorant, ‘near’ to the wise. The simultaneity of antar and bāhyataḥ refutes any spatial conception."],["Sivananda","Meditate on the verse line by line — hold the simultaneity until the spatial language collapses into direct intuition."],["Krishnananda","The deepest verse of the first eight. A structured demolition of every either-or that the mind tries to apply to the Ātman. The four pairs (move/not-move; far/near; within/outside) are the four canonical categories under which mind tries to grasp the Real, and each is denied by being affirmed in its opposite."],["Deussen","The closest Upaniṣadic parallel is Muṇḍaka 2.1.2 (‘apāṇi-pādo javano grahītā’). Reads tat ejati tan naijati as the literary prototype of Bhagavad-Gītā 13.15–16."],["Aurobindo","The seed of the integral Vedānta. Brahman is not the negation of motion (an inert Absolute) and not exhausted by motion (a pure becoming); it is the simultaneous reality of both — that is what tat ejati tan naijati means as a non-elliptical statement."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 6","title":"The seer of all-in-Self does not recoil","sa":["यस्तु सर्वाणि भूतान्यात्मन्येवानुपश्यति ।","सर्वभूतेषु चात्मानं ततो न विजुगुप्सते ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["yas tu sarvāṇi bhūtāny ātmany evānupaśyati |","sarva-bhūteṣu cātmānaṃ tato na vijugupsate || 6 ||"],"words":[["yaḥ","pron. m. nom. sg.","who"],["tu","indecl.","but / however"],["sarvāṇi","n. acc. pl.","all"],["bhūtāni","n. acc. pl.","beings"],["ātmani","m. loc. sg.","in the Self"],["eva","indecl. emph.","only"],["anu-paśyati","3 sg. pres. of anu-paś","sees (insight-after-instruction)"],["sarva-bhūteṣu","n. loc. pl.","in all beings"],["ca","conj.","and"],["ātmānam","m. acc. sg.","the Self"],["tataḥ","adv.","from that / therefore"],["na vi-jugupsate","neg. + 3 sg. pres. ātm. desid. of vi-gup","recoils not"]],"lit":"“But he who sees all beings in the Self alone, and the Self in all beings — from that, he does not recoil.”","para":"The one who sees everything within himself, and himself within everything, has nothing to recoil from. The very basis of revulsion is annulled.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> anu-paśyati = ‘sees following (the teaching of śruti)’ — not self-generated insight but the same vision the seers had. The not-recoiling is not stoicism but the natural cessation of the ground of revulsion."],["Sivananda","Practical universal love. The verse is the scriptural basis of Sivananda’s well-known triad ‘Adapt, adjust, accommodate.’"],["Krishnananda","The verse undoes the subject-object structure that revulsion presupposes. Aversion needs another; if there is no other, there is no aversion. The verse is therefore not an ethical exhortation but an ontological consequence."],["Deussen","Parallel Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.4.23 (‘ātmanaivātmānaṃ paśyati’). Marks this as the canonical Vedāntic non-dual ethics."],["Aurobindo","The practical face of mantra 5. The simultaneity-of-far-and-near in mantra 5 becomes the simultaneity-of-self-and-other in the seer. There is no longer a ‘them’ to recoil from."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 7","title":"Where unity is seen, no delusion, no grief","sa":["यस्मिन्सर्वाणि भूतान्यात्मैवाभूद्विजानतः ।","तत्र को मोहः कः शोक एकत्वमनुपश्यतः ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["yasmin sarvāṇi bhūtāny ātmaivābhūd vijānataḥ |","tatra ko mohaḥ kaḥ śoka ekatvam anupaśyataḥ || 7 ||"],"words":[["yasmin","pron. m. loc. sg.","in whom / for whom"],["sarvāṇi bhūtāni","n. nom. pl.","all beings"],["ātmā","m. nom. sg.","the Self"],["eva","indecl. emph.","only"],["abhūt","3 sg. impf. of √bhū","has become"],["vi-jānataḥ","m. gen. sg. pap. of vi-jñā","for the knower"],["tatra","adv.","there"],["kaḥ mohaḥ","pron. + m. nom. sg.","what delusion"],["kaḥ śokaḥ","pron. + m. nom. sg.","what grief"],["ekatvam","n. acc. sg.","oneness"],["anu-paśyataḥ","m. gen. sg. pap.","of the one seeing-after"]],"lit":"“For the knower in whom all beings have become the Self itself — there, what delusion, what grief, for one seeing the One-ness?”","para":"For the one who has seen that all beings are the Self — what room is there for confusion or sorrow? Both presuppose duality, and there is none.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> Completes the argument of mantra 6. Once all-beings = Self, the structural conditions for moha (mistaking one thing for another) and śoka (loss of one thing relative to another) are absent."],["Sivananda","An instruction for the bereaved — Sivananda is reported to have recited this verse at the funerals of ashramites."],["Krishnananda","śoka here is more general than ‘grief’ — it is the whole phenomenology of lack. Where there is no second to be lost, there is no lack."],["Deussen","Parallels Chāndogya 7.1.3 (Nārada’s grief). Reads mantras 6–7 as a single argument with mantra 5 supplying the premise."],["Aurobindo","The verse states what the realised state actually feels like: not the absence of phenomena (delusion and grief are still possible-as-content) but the absence of their grip. They cannot wound the one who knows."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 8","title":"The Self has encompassed all — bright, bodiless, woundless","sa":["स पर्यगाच्छुक्रमकायमव्रणमस्नाविरꣳ शुद्धमपापविद्धम् ।","कविर्मनीषी परिभूः स्वयम्भूर्याथातथ्यतोऽर्थान्व्यदधाच्छाश्वतीभ्यः समाभ्यः ॥ ८ ॥"],"iast":["sa paryagāc chukram akāyam avraṇam asnāviraṃ śuddham apāpa-viddham |","kavir manīṣī paribhūḥ svayambhūr yāthātathyato ’rthān vyadadhāc chāśvatībhyaḥ samābhyaḥ || 8 ||"],"words":[["saḥ","pron. m. nom. sg.","he / It"],["pari-agāt","3 sg. impf. of pari-gā/i","encompassed / went around all"],["śukram","n. acc. sg.","radiant / bright"],["a-kāyam","n. acc. sg.","bodiless"],["a-vraṇam","n. acc. sg.","woundless"],["a-snāviram","n. acc. sg.","sinewless"],["śuddham","n. acc. sg.","pure"],["a-pāpa-viddham","n. acc. sg. (kc.)","un-pierced by evil"],["kaviḥ","m. nom. sg.","seer (poet)"],["manīṣī","m. nom. sg.","thinker"],["paribhūḥ","m. nom. sg.","all-encompassing"],["svayam-bhūḥ","m. nom. sg.","self-existent"],["yāthātathyataḥ","adv.","according to reality / according to need"],["arthān","m. acc. pl.","objects / purposes"],["vy-adadhāt","3 sg. impf. of vi-dhā","distributed / disposed"],["śāśvatībhyaḥ samābhyaḥ","f. dat. pl.","for the everlasting years"]],"lit":"“He has encompassed (all): radiant, bodiless, woundless, sinewless, pure, untouched by evil; the seer, the thinker, the all-encompassing, the self-existent — he disposed objects rightly for the everlasting years.”","para":"The Self pervades all. It has no body, no wound, no sinew; it is pure, untouched by evil. As seer, thinker, encompasser, and self-born one, it has allotted to each thing its proper nature, forever.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The most concentrated verse of the Upaniṣad — eight epithets of nirguṇa Brahman in two pādas. The closing verb (vyadadhāt) is the only verse in the Īśā where Brahman is described as acting; read as a perfectly natural ordering, with no agentive striving."],["Sivananda","Meditate on each of the eight epithets in turn — they are the canonical Upaniṣadic ālambanas for nirguṇa-dhyāna."],["Krishnananda","The Īśā’s own creed; cf. the eight-fold formulation in Muṇḍaka 1.1.6. The closing pāda (svayambhūḥ … śāśvatībhyaḥ samābhyaḥ) does not deny the active aspect of Brahman; rather it integrates it with the negative epithets above."],["Deussen","Marks this as the most archaic-feeling verse of the Īśā, with metrical traces of Saṃhitā-period composition. The pāpa-viddham phrase echoes Atharvaveda 10.7."],["Aurobindo","The climactic verse of the cosmological section (mantras 1–8). All eight epithets are simultaneous descriptions of the One that, in mantra 1, is to be ‘worn.’ This is what Īśa is — and yet the next verses will say that he requires both vidyā and avidyā to be reached."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 9","title":"Worshippers of avidyā enter blind darkness; of vidyā, greater darkness","sa":["अन्धं तमः प्रविशन्ति येऽविद्यामुपासते ।","ततो भूय इव ते तमो य उ विद्यायाꣳ रताः ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["andhaṃ tamaḥ praviśanti ye ’vidyām upāsate |","tato bhūya iva te tamo ya u vidyāyāṃ ratāḥ || 9 ||"],"words":[["andham tamaḥ","n. acc. sg.","blind/blinding darkness"],["pra-viśanti","3 pl. pres. of pra-viś","they enter"],["ye","pron. m. nom. pl.","those who"],["avidyām","f. acc. sg.","non-knowledge / ignorance (ritual-action alone)"],["upāsate","3 pl. pres. ātm. of upa-ās","worship / cultivate"],["tataḥ","adv.","than that"],["bhūyaḥ","adv. compar.","more"],["iva","indecl.","as it were"],["te","pron. m. nom. pl.","they"],["ye u","rel. + emph.","those who indeed"],["vidyāyām","f. loc. sg.","in knowledge (devata-jñāna alone)"],["ratāḥ","m. nom. pl. pp. of ram","engrossed / delighted"]],"lit":"“Into blind darkness they enter who worship avidyā; into greater darkness, as it were, those who delight (only) in vidyā.”","para":"Those who cultivate only ignorance enter blind darkness. But those who cultivate only knowledge enter a darkness greater still.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The most controverted verse in the Upaniṣad. Śaṅkara takes ‘vidyā’ here NOT as ātma-jñāna (which could never lead to greater darkness) but as devata-jñāna (worship of finite deities). Hierarchy: ritual (avidyā) — bad; deity-worship without ritual (vidyā as one-sided) — worse; both together (mantra 11) — good. This rescues the higher meaning of vidyā as Self-knowledge from the verse’s apparent rejection."],["Sivananda","Warns that mere book-learning, without action, is worse than mere action without learning. The intellectual who never acts is more lost than the actor who never reflects."],["Krishnananda","Rejects the Śaṅkara reading. For Krishnananda the verse means what it says — even genuine knowledge, cultivated in isolation from action, becomes a deeper trap than ignorance, because it produces a subtler ego."],["Deussen","Parallels Bṛhadāraṇyaka 4.4.10 (almost verbatim). Reads the verse as polemical against the early renouncer movements that scorned the ritualists."],["Aurobindo","The central pivot. Avidyā here is the Many; Vidyā here is the One. Cultivate either alone, and you are lost — to materialism in the first case, to acosmism in the second. The synthesis (mantra 11) is the whole point."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 10","title":"One fruit by vidyā, another by avidyā — so we heard","sa":["अन्यदेवाहुर्विद्ययाऽन्यदाहुरविद्यया ।","इति शुश्रुम धीराणां ये नस्तद्विचचक्षिरे ॥ १० ॥"],"iast":["anyad evāhur vidyayānyad āhur avidyayā |","iti śuśruma dhīrāṇāṃ ye nas tad vicacakṣire || 10 ||"],"words":[["anyat","pron. n. acc. sg.","one (thing) / something else"],["eva","emph.","indeed"],["āhuḥ","3 pl. pf. of √ah","they have said"],["vidyayā","f. instr. sg.","by vidyā"],["anyat","pron. n.","(another) thing"],["āhuḥ","they have said",""],["avidyayā","f. instr. sg.","by avidyā"],["iti","quotative","thus"],["śuśruma","1 pl. pf. of √śru","we have heard"],["dhīrāṇām","m. gen. pl.","of the wise"],["ye","rel. m. nom. pl.","who"],["naḥ","pron. acc. pl. enclitic","to us"],["tat","pron. n. acc. sg.","that"],["vi-cacakṣire","3 pl. pf. ātm. of vi-cakṣ","expounded"]],"lit":"“‘One thing is said (to be attained) by vidyā, another by avidyā’ — thus have we heard from the wise, who expounded that to us.”","para":"Knowledge and ignorance, the wise have said, lead to different fruits. We have heard it from the masters.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> ‘Another by vidyā’ = devata-prāpti (attainment of deity-status); ‘another by avidyā’ = the result of ritual (e.g., svarga-loka). Two genuinely different fruits."],["Sivananda","The verse is śruti citing śruti — the canonical move of Upaniṣadic conservatism. Sivananda often noted that the Īśā here demonstrates how the Upaniṣads themselves model the role of received tradition."],["Krishnananda","The parallel structure with mantra 13 (anyad evāhuḥ sambhavāt) shows that the Upaniṣad is consciously building a four-term framework (vidyā / avidyā / sambhūti / asambhūti)."],["Deussen","Standard śuśruma-formula introducing received teaching; same syntactical pattern as Kena 4.3. Notes that the appeal to dhīrāḥ marks this as a quotation, not the Upaniṣad’s own first-person doctrine."],["Aurobindo","The ‘different fruit’ of vidyā is the immortality-of-the-One; the ‘different fruit’ of avidyā is the transcendence-of-death-in-the-Many. Both are real, and the next mantra will synthesise them."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 11","title":"Knowing vidyā and avidyā together — crossing death, attaining immortality","sa":["विद्यां चाविद्यां च यस्तद्वेदोभयꣳ सह ।","अविद्यया मृत्युं तीर्त्वा विद्ययाऽमृतमश्नुते ॥ ११ ॥"],"iast":["vidyāṃ cāvidyāṃ ca yas tad vedobhayaṃ saha |","avidyayā mṛtyuṃ tīrtvā vidyayāmṛtam aśnute || 11 ||"],"words":[["vidyām","f. acc. sg.","knowledge"],["ca","conj.","and"],["avidyām","f. acc. sg.","non-knowledge"],["yaḥ","pron. m. nom. sg.","who"],["tat","pron. n. acc. sg.","that"],["veda","3 sg. pf. (perfectival pres.) of √vid","knows"],["ubhayam","n. acc. sg.","both"],["saha","indecl.","together"],["avidyayā","f. instr. sg.","by avidyā"],["mṛtyum","m. acc. sg.","death"],["tīrtvā","ger. of √tṝ","having crossed"],["vidyayā","f. instr. sg.","by vidyā"],["amṛtam","n. acc. sg.","the deathless / immortality"],["aśnute","3 sg. pres. ātm. of √aś","enjoys / attains"]],"lit":"“He who knows both vidyā and avidyā together — having crossed death by avidyā, he enjoys immortality by vidyā.”","para":"The one who holds knowledge and ignorance together crosses death through (the right use of) ignorance, and through (the right use of) knowledge attains immortality.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The resolution. The karma-niṣṭha crosses death (= the round of birth-and-death) by avidyā (= karma rightly done with devata-jñāna) and then attains amṛtam (= deity-status, the highest provisional good available without jñāna). The famous Śaṅkara reading that resists karma-jñāna-samuccaya by reading vidyā here as devata-jñāna, not Self-knowledge."],["Sivananda","Live the householder’s life as the path itself: karma (avidyā) for crossing the world, jñāna (vidyā) for crossing the self. The hyphenated path."],["Krishnananda","The actual karma-jñāna-samuccaya verse, against Śaṅkara. Karma is genuinely transformative; jñāna without it is sterile; the two together are the path."],["Deussen","The canonical Vedāntic compromise. Reads it historically as the Upaniṣad’s response to the karma-vs-jñāna debate already raging in the late Brāhmaṇa period."],["Aurobindo","The central thesis of the Īśā. Death (the limitation of the Many) is crossed by the Many themselves rightly understood (avidyā); immortality (the freedom of the One) is attained by the One known (vidyā). Neither alone suffices."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 12","title":"Worshippers of asambhūti enter blind darkness; of sambhūti, greater darkness","sa":["अन्धं तमः प्रविशन्ति येऽसम्भूतिमुपासते ।","ततो भूय इव ते तमो य उ सम्भूत्याꣳ रताः ॥ १२ ॥"],"iast":["andhaṃ tamaḥ praviśanti ye ’sambhūtim upāsate |","tato bhūya iva te tamo ya u sambhūtyāṃ ratāḥ || 12 ||"],"words":[["andham tamaḥ","n. acc. sg.","blind darkness"],["pra-viśanti","3 pl. pres.","they enter"],["ye","rel. m. nom. pl.","those who"],["a-sambhūtim","f. acc. sg.","the un-become / the unmanifest (avyakta / prakṛti)"],["upāsate","3 pl. pres. ātm.","worship"],["tataḥ bhūyaḥ iva","adv. + adv. + indecl.","greater than that, as it were"],["tamaḥ","n. acc. sg.","darkness"],["ye u","rel. + emph.","those who indeed"],["sambhūtyām","f. loc. sg.","in the become / the manifest (Hiraṇyagarbha / kārya-brahman)"],["ratāḥ","m. nom. pl. pp.","engrossed"]],"lit":"“Into blind darkness they enter who worship asambhūti (the unmanifest); into greater darkness, as it were, those who delight (only) in sambhūti (the manifest).”","para":"Worship only the Unbecome and you enter darkness. Worship only the Become and the darkness is greater still.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> A major reading-problem. Śaṅkara takes asambhūti as prakṛti (un-manifest matter) and sambhūti as Hiraṇyagarbha (manifest cosmic God). Verse: worshippers of prakṛti enter darkness; worshippers of Hiraṇyagarbha (a finite god, however cosmic) enter greater darkness."],["Sivananda","Warns equally against (a) the materialist who worships brute nature, and (b) the mythologist who worships any limited deity. Both are partial; both bind."],["Krishnananda","Takes asambhūti = the unmanifest principle (avyakta) and sambhūti = the manifest universe; both, isolated, are partial. Reads this verse as a metaphysical doublet of mantra 9 — vidyā/avidyā is the epistemological axis, sambhūti/asambhūti is the ontological axis."],["Deussen","Notes that Śaṅkara’s prakṛti / Hiraṇyagarbha reading is anachronistic — Sāṃkhya vocabulary postdates the Īśā. The Upaniṣad’s own terms are simpler: the non-manifest and the manifest, conceived non-technically."],["Aurobindo","The ontological face of mantras 9–11. The unmanifest alone is barren (a kind of Buddhist śūnyatā avant la lettre); the manifest alone is the round of suffering. Both together, integrally known, are the Real."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 13","title":"One fruit from sambhava, another from asambhava — so we heard","sa":["अन्यदेवाहुः सम्भवादन्यदाहुरसम्भवात् ।","इति शुश्रुम धीराणां ये नस्तद्विचचक्षिरे ॥ १३ ॥"],"iast":["anyad evāhuḥ sambhavād anyad āhur asambhavāt |","iti śuśruma dhīrāṇāṃ ye nas tad vicacakṣire || 13 ||"],"words":[["anyat","pron. n. acc. sg.","one (thing) / something else"],["eva","emph.","indeed"],["āhuḥ","3 pl. pf.","they have said"],["sambhavāt","m. abl. sg.","from sambhava (origination)"],["anyat","pron. n.","(another) thing"],["āhuḥ","they have said",""],["a-sambhavāt","m. abl. sg.","from asambhava (non-origination)"],["iti śuśruma","quotative + 1 pl. pf.","thus we have heard"],["dhīrāṇām","m. gen. pl.","of the wise"],["ye naḥ tat vi-cacakṣire","rel. + pron. + pron. + 3 pl. pf.","who expounded that to us"]],"lit":"“‘One thing is said (to result) from sambhava, another from asambhava’ — thus have we heard from the wise, who expounded that to us.”","para":"From origination one fruit, from non-origination another — so said the masters.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> sambhava = origination of the manifest god (Hiraṇyagarbha); asambhava = (worship of) the unmanifest matter. Different fruits accordingly."],["Sivananda","The deliberate parallelism with mantra 10 marks the Īśā’s love of the four-term schema; do not skim over the repetition — it is the structural argument."],["Krishnananda","Again rejects Śaṅkara’s prakṛti-Hiraṇyagarbha reading. The ‘different fruits’ are different misconceptions, both still partial; only the synthesis (mantra 14) is the truth."],["Deussen","Parallel construction marks a poetic-doctrinal couplet; the verse-pair 12–14 is rhetorically a duplicate of 9–11 but ontological where the first is epistemological."],["Aurobindo","This verse marks that the dharma of the Many has its own fruit (transcendence-of-suffering), the dharma of the One has its own fruit (immortality-of-form); mantra 14 will integrate them."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 14","title":"Knowing sambhūti and vināśa together — crossing death, attaining immortality","sa":["सम्भूतिं च विनाशं च यस्तद्वेदोभयꣳ सह ।","विनाशेन मृत्युं तीर्त्वा सम्भूत्याऽमृतमश्नुते ॥ १४ ॥"],"iast":["sambhūtiṃ ca vināśaṃ ca yas tad vedobhayaṃ saha |","vināśena mṛtyuṃ tīrtvā sambhūtyāmṛtam aśnute || 14 ||"],"words":[["sambhūtim","f. acc. sg.","the become / the manifest"],["ca","conj.","and"],["vināśam","m. acc. sg.","destruction (= asambhūti in Kāṇva)"],["yaḥ tat veda","rel. + pron. + 3 sg. pf.","who knows that"],["ubhayam saha","n. acc. sg. + indecl.","both together"],["vināśena","m. instr. sg.","by destruction (Mādhyandina); Kāṇva: asambhūtyā by the unmanifest"],["mṛtyum tīrtvā","m. acc. sg. + ger.","having crossed death"],["sambhūtyā","f. instr. sg.","by the manifest"],["amṛtam aśnute","n. acc. sg. + 3 sg. pres.","attains immortality"]],"lit":"“He who knows sambhūti and vināśa (or: asambhūti) together — having crossed death by vināśa, he attains immortality by sambhūti.”","para":"The one who holds the Manifest and the Unmanifest (or: Destruction) together crosses death by the latter and attains immortality by the former.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The resolution of the second pair, parallel to mantra 11. By worship of the unmanifest one crosses death (i.e., transcends the gross body); by worship of the manifest Hiraṇyagarbha one attains the deity’s amṛta-status. The variant ‘vināśena’ (instead of ‘asambhūtyā’) is glossed as ‘the perishable’ = the embodied result of merit-karma, by which one crosses ordinary death."],["Sivananda","The unmanifest is the seed-state out of which the manifest arises; cultivate awareness of both transitions in daily sādhanā."],["Krishnananda","Again the karma-jñāna-samuccaya reading: real-world action (sambhūti) and contemplation of the unmanifest (asambhūti) are both required for the integral life."],["Deussen","Marks the textual problem (vināśena vs asambhūtyā) and reads the variant as Mādhyandina-school grammatical clarification. Treats the verse as completing the doctrinal coupling."],["Aurobindo","The immortality attained ‘by sambhūti’ is not paradoxical — it is the immortality of the Many seen as the One, the Īśā’s deepest teaching. To deny the manifest in pursuit of the unmanifest is to lose both."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 15","title":"The face of Truth covered with a golden disc — O Pūṣan, uncover it","sa":["हिरण्मयेन पात्रेण सत्यस्यापिहितं मुखम् ।","तत्त्वं पूषन्नपावृणु सत्यधर्माय दृष्टये ॥ १५ ॥"],"iast":["hiraṇmayena pātreṇa satyasyāpihitaṃ mukham |","tat tvaṃ pūṣann apāvṛṇu satya-dharmāya dṛṣṭaye || 15 ||"],"words":[["hiraṇmayena","n. instr. sg.","(with) golden"],["pātreṇa","n. instr. sg.","(with a) disc / vessel"],["satyasya","m./n. gen. sg.","of Truth"],["api-hitam","n. nom. sg. pp. of api-dhā","covered"],["mukham","n. nom. sg.","face / mouth"],["tat tvam","pron. n. acc. sg. + pron. nom. sg.","that, you"],["pūṣan","m. voc. sg.","O Pūṣan (sun-god)"],["apa-āvṛṇu","2 sg. impv. of apa-vṛ","uncover"],["satya-dharmāya","m. dat. sg.","for the one whose dharma is truth"],["dṛṣṭaye","f. dat. sg.","for seeing"]],"lit":"“The face of Truth is covered with a golden disc. That, O Pūṣan, uncover — for seeing, for the one whose dharma is truth.”","para":"A golden cover hides the face of Truth. O Pūṣan (Sun!), draw it aside — that I, the truth-keeper, may see.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The ‘golden disc’ is the resplendent maṇḍala of the visible sun, which both reveals (by light) and conceals (by glare) the inner Puruṣa. The seeker requests removal so the Puruṣa within may be seen directly. The canonical sūryopāsanā passage of the Upaniṣadic corpus."],["Sivananda","Practical sūryopāsanā — recite this mantra at sunrise, gazing at the rising sun with reverence (Sivananda explicitly warns against direct sun-gazing; the practice is at first or last light, eyes barely open)."],["Krishnananda","The golden disc is ‘the brightness of objectivity’ — the very visibility of the world that hides the Self behind the world. The prayer is to drop the world’s brightness so that the silent Real can show."],["Deussen","Notes the verse’s ritual origin as a sandhya-mantra; older than its Upaniṣadic context. The dying man at sunset would have used it; the Īśā has placed it in the eschatological closing block."],["Aurobindo","The most striking image of the Upaniṣad. The Sun is Truth itself; the disc is the very form by which Truth appears. The prayer is for the form to step aside that the Formless behind the Form may be seen — which the next mantra answers with so’ham asmi."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 16","title":"O Pūṣan, gather your rays — that Person yonder, I am He","sa":["पूषन्नेकर्षे यम सूर्य प्राजापत्य व्यूह रश्मीन्समूह तेजः ।","यत्ते रूपं कल्याणतमं तत्ते पश्यामि योऽसावसौ पुरुषः सोऽहमस्मि ॥ १६ ॥"],"iast":["pūṣann ekarṣe yama sūrya prājāpatya vyūha raśmīn samūha tejaḥ |","yat te rūpaṃ kalyāṇatamaṃ tat te paśyāmi yo ’sāv asau puruṣaḥ so ’ham asmi || 16 ||"],"words":[["pūṣan","m. voc. sg.","O Pūṣan (Nourisher)"],["ekarṣe","m. voc. sg.","O sole-traveller / lone seer"],["yama","m. voc. sg.","O Yama (Restrainer / Death)"],["sūrya","m. voc. sg.","O Sūrya"],["prājāpatya","m. voc. sg.","O son-of-Prajāpati"],["vy-ūha","2 sg. impv. of vi-ūh","disperse / arrange"],["raśmīn","m. acc. pl.","rays"],["sam-ūha","2 sg. impv. of sam-ūh","gather"],["tejaḥ","n. acc. sg.","splendour / heat"],["yat te rūpam","rel. n. + pron. gen. + n. nom. sg.","that form of yours which"],["kalyāṇa-tamam","n. nom. sg. superl.","most-auspicious"],["tat te paśyāmi","pron. n. + pron. + 1 sg. pres.","that of you I see"],["yo ’sāv asau puruṣaḥ","rel. + pron. + pron. + m. nom. sg.","whoever is that very Puruṣa yonder"],["so ’ham asmi","pron. m. + pron. + 1 sg. pres. of √as","He, I am"]],"lit":"“O Pūṣan, sole seer, Yama, Sūrya, son of Prajāpati — disperse your rays, gather (in) your splendour; that most-auspicious form of yours, that I see (of you). Whoever is that Puruṣa yonder, I am He.”","para":"O Sun (under all your names!), pull in your rays and concentrate your glow. The blessed form of yours, that I now see. That Person who is yonder — I myself am He.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The climactic moment of the eschatological block. The five vocatives (Pūṣan, Ekarṣe, Yama, Sūrya, Prājāpatya) are five names of the same deity, addressed from five aspects. ‘yo ’sāv asau puruṣaḥ so ’ham asmi’ is the Īśā’s mahā-vākya — the equivalent of ‘tat tvam asi’ of the Chāndogya and ‘ayam ātmā brahma’ of the Māṇḍūkya."],["Sivananda","Practical reading: meditate on the Self in the sun-disc; let the rays gather; let the form become inward; rest in ‘so’ham.’ A complete morning-and-evening sādhanā in one verse."],["Krishnananda","‘so’ham asmi’ here is not a flat identity but a recognition: the Puruṣa addressed as ‘yonder’ (asau) is recognised as the one addressing — the apparently external is realised as the self-same Self."],["Deussen","Parallels Chāndogya 1.6.6 (‘ya eṣo ’ntar āditye hiraṇmayaḥ puruṣaḥ’). Treats this as the Upaniṣad’s most explicit identification of the cosmic sun-puruṣa with the inner ātman."],["Aurobindo","The heart of the Īśā. The fivefold vocative is not five names of one being but the five faces of the integral truth — nourisher, sole-traveller, restrainer, illuminator, son of the Father. To know one is to know all five; to know all five is to know oneself as the Person seen."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 17","title":"The dying man’s prayer (Mādhyandina ∥ Kāṇva)","sa":[],"words":[["vāyuḥ","m. nom. sg.","(the individual) breath, the wind"],["anilam","m. acc. sg.","the wind (cosmic air)"],["amṛtam","n. acc. sg.","immortal / undying"],["atha","indecl.","now / and"],["idam śarīram","pron. n. nom. sg. + n. nom. sg.","this body"],["bhasma-antam","n. nom. sg. (bahuvrīhi)","(having) ash-as-end"],["oṃ","syll.","Oṃ (the praṇava)"],["krato","m. voc. sg. of kratu","O Resolve / O purposive will"],["smara","2 sg. impv. of √smṛ","remember"],["kṛtam","n. acc. sg. pp. of √kṛ","(what was) done"],["smara","2 sg. impv.","remember"]],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The dying man’s prayer, offered at the threshold of death by the karma-niṣṭha (the one of mantra 2). ‘Krato’ = the will-as-faculty, the agent of resolutions. ‘kṛtam smara’ = ‘remember what was done’ — i.e., remember the karma that brought you here, so that its fruit may attach properly; cf. the doctrine of antya-smaraṇa in the Gītā 8.6."],["Sivananda","The canonical antya-smaraṇa-mantra. Sivananda taught his disciples to chant this at the deathbed of relatives, holding the dying person’s hand, repeating slowly so the departing mind hears."],["Krishnananda","Not merely a deathbed prayer but the existential structure of every moment: each breath returns to amṛta, each cell becomes ash, and the will is constantly asked to remember its acts. The mantra makes mortality continuous, not punctiliar."],["Deussen","Notes this as the oldest stratum of the Īśā; clearly a pre-existing funeral mantra, incorporated into the Upaniṣad in the late composition phase. The Kāṇva single-recitation is the original; the Mādhyandina doubling is for liturgical emphasis. Cites Atharvaveda 18.2 parallels in the funeral hymns."],["Aurobindo","The breath, the body, the will — all three of which the verse mentions — are the three pillars of Aurobindo’s psychology. Breath returns to its source; body to its source; will alone, integrally remembered, carries forward as the soul’s record."]],"notes":["This is the most textually variant verse of the Īśā. The Mādhyandina recension doubles the closing imperative ‘oṃ krato smara kṛtaṃ smara’; the Kāṇva states it once. The opening pāda is substantially identical. Both readings are reproduced below.","<table><tr><td>Mādhyandina recension</td><td>Kāṇva recension</td></tr><tr><td>वायुरनिलममृतमथेदं भस्मान्तꣳ शरीरम् ।<br>ॐ क्रतो स्मर कृतꣳ स्मर क्रतो स्मर कृतꣳ स्मर ॥ १७ ॥</td><td>वायुरनिलममृतमथेदं भस्मान्तꣳ शरीरम् ।<br>ॐ क्रतो स्मर कृतꣳ स्मर ॥ १७ ॥</td></tr><tr><td>vāyur anilam amṛtam athedaṃ bhasmāntaṃ śarīram |<br>oṃ krato smara kṛtaṃ smara krato smara kṛtaṃ smara || 17 ||</td><td>vāyur anilam amṛtam athedaṃ bhasmāntaṃ śarīram |<br>oṃ krato smara kṛtaṃ smara || 17 ||</td></tr></table>","Literal: “(May) the breath (go to) the immortal cosmic wind; and this body (end) in ashes. Oṃ, O Resolve, remember; remember what was done. (Mādhyandina repeats: O Resolve, remember; remember what was done.)”","Paraphrase: Let my breath dissolve into the undying air; let this body end in ash. Oṃ — purposive will, remember! Remember all that was done. (Remember once more!)"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Mantra 18","title":"O Agni, lead us by the good path (Mādhyandina ≡ Kāṇva)","sa":["अग्ने नय सुपथा राये अस्मान् विश्वानि देव वयुनानि विद्वान् ।","युयोध्यस्मज्जुहुराणमेनो भूयिष्ठां ते नमउक्तिं विधेम ॥ १८ ॥"],"iast":["agne naya supathā rāye asmān viśvāni deva vayunāni vidvān |","yuyodhy asmaj juhurāṇam eno bhūyiṣṭhāṃ te nama-uktiṃ vidhema || 18 ||"],"words":[["agne","m. voc. sg.","O Agni"],["naya","2 sg. impv. of √nī","lead"],["su-pathā","m. instr. sg.","by the good path"],["rāye","m. dat. sg. of rai","for wealth / for the (true) good"],["asmān","pron. acc. pl.","us"],["viśvāni","n. acc. pl.","all"],["deva","m. voc. sg.","O god"],["vayunāni","n. acc. pl. of vayuna","ways / acts / knowings"],["vidvān","m. nom. sg. perf. act. part. of √vid","knowing"],["yuyodhi","2 sg. impv. of √yu","drive away / separate (us from)"],["asmat","pron. abl. pl.","from us"],["juhurāṇam","n. acc. sg. pap. of hvṛ","winding / crooked"],["enaḥ","n. acc. sg.","sin / wrong"],["bhūyiṣṭhām","f. acc. sg. superl.","most-abundant"],["te","pron. dat./gen. sg.","to you"],["namas-uktim","f. acc. sg. (compound)","speech-of-homage"],["vidhema","1 pl. pot. of vi-dhā","may we offer"]],"lit":"“O Agni, lead us by the good path to the (true) good — O god knowing all our ways — drive from us the crooked sin. May we offer to you the most-abundant speech of homage.”","para":"O Agni, you who know all our acts — lead us by the straight path to true wealth, drive away our winding wrongs. We offer you again and again our most copious homage.","coms":[["Gambhirananda","<em>(Śaṅkara)</em> The closing prayer of the Upaniṣad. Spoken by the same dying ātman whose breath is dissolving (mantra 17), now addressing Agni — the inner fire that will carry the resolved-and-remembered will onward. ‘Su-pathā’ = the devayāna, the path of the gods, in contrast to the pitṛyāna of Chāndogya 5.10."],["Sivananda","Chant at the close of every spiritual session; it seals the practice with the prayer for guidance and purification. Sivananda always concluded his weekly satsaṅgs with this verse."],["Krishnananda","Agni is here the immanent divine fire — not the ritual fire but the inner principle that knows all acts. The prayer for su-pathā is the prayer not to lose, in dying, the orientation gathered in living."],["Deussen","Parallel Ṛgveda 1.189.1 — the Upaniṣad has appropriated a Ṛgvedic verse intact. The ‘su-pathā’ is the same path that, in the Ṛgveda, is the way to the gods. Marks this as the only such verbatim Ṛgvedic incorporation in the principal-Upaniṣad corpus."],["Aurobindo","The closing mantra integrates the entire Upaniṣad: the seer asks to be led — i.e., enacts mantra 2 (kurvann eveha); ‘for the (true) wealth’ (rāye) — i.e., enacts mantra 1 (mā gṛdhaḥ kasya svid dhanam, reread now as gṛdhaḥ towards the true dhana of Brahman); by removing crookedness — i.e., enacts mantras 9–14 (the integration that drives away one-sidedness); through speech-of-homage — i.e., enacts the recognition of mantra 16 (so’ham asmi, now phrased not as identity-claim but as the praise that is the form of the identity). The Upaniṣad ends, like a great symphony, recapitulating every theme."]],"notes":["Mantra 18 is identical in both recensions; it is in fact Ṛgveda 1.189.1 quoted verbatim, the only Ṛgvedic verse that is incorporated into the principal Upaniṣads in unchanged form."]},{"ref":"Visual Representations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Five ASCII diagrams of the Īśā’s geometry. Each is a structural reading of one section of the text. Together they trace the Upaniṣad’s architecture from the Lord-clothed cosmos (mantra 1) to the dying man’s ascent on the good path (mantras 17–18)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 1","title":"Īśā vāsyam: the cosmos as the Lord’s garment","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>              ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n              │                  Ī Ś A                          │  ← the Lord\n              │   ┌───────────── vāsya ─────────────┐           │     (mantra 1)\n              │   │                                 │           │\n              │   │       J A G A T  in  jagatī     │           │  ← all-moving\n              │   │   ┌──────────┐   ┌──────────┐   │           │     in moving\n              │   │   │ subject  │   │  object  │   │           │     (locus)\n              │   │   │  (you)   │←──│ (wealth) │   │           │\n              │   │   └────┬─────┘   └────┬─────┘   │           │\n              │   │        │              │         │           │\n              │   │        ▼              ▼         │           │\n              │   │   bhuñjīthā    mā gṛdhaḥ        │           │  ← enjoy / not covet\n              │   │   (with tena tyaktena)          │           │\n              │   └─────────────────────────────────┘           │\n              │                                                 │\n              └─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                    The entire frame is Īśa-vāsyam.\n                    Renunciation is the *medium* of enjoyment,\n                    not its opposite.</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 2","title":"Mantra 5: the four refused dichotomies","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>                                tad ejati\n                                    │\n                                    │\n              dūre  ──────┐  ┌──────── antike\n                          │  │\n                  ─── ─── BRAHMAN ─── ─── ─── (no spatial term applies)\n                          │  │\n              antar ──────┘  └──────── bāhyataḥ\n                                    │\n                                    │\n                                tan naijati\n\n              Each pair (moves/not-moves, far/near, within/outside)\n              is the canonical category under which mind tries to\n              grasp the Real, and the Upaniṣad refutes each one\n              by AFFIRMING it in its opposite.</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 3","title":"Mantras 9–14: the four-term resolution","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>                        EPISTEMIC AXIS (mantras 9–11)\n                        ─────────────────────────────\n                          avidyā   ←────►   vidyā\n                            │                │\n                            │                │\n                            ▼                ▼\n                       (crossing         (attaining\n                          death)        immortality)\n                            \\              /\n                             \\            /\n                              \\          /\n                               \\        /\n                                \\      /\n                                 SAHA          (= mantra 11)\n                              &quot;ubhayam saha&quot;\n                                 /      \\\n                                /        \\\n                               /          \\\n                              /            \\\n                             /              \\\n                            ▼                ▼\n                       (crossing         (attaining\n                          death)        immortality)\n                            ▲                ▲\n                            │                │\n                            │                │\n                       asambhūti  ←────►  sambhūti\n                       (≡ vināśa)\n                        ─────────────────────────────\n                        ONTOLOGICAL AXIS (mantras 12–14)\n\n              Both axes converge on a SINGLE resolution: hold\n              both poles together. The integration is what the\n              Upaniṣad calls *amṛtam*. Either pole alone is\n              *andhaṃ tamaḥ*.</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 4","title":"Mantra 16: the fivefold Sun = the inner Self","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>                        ┌─── Pūṣan      (Nourisher)\n                        │\n                        ├─── Ekarṣe     (Sole traveller)\n                        │\n              ONE  ─────┼─── Yama       (Restrainer / Death)         ←  THE\n              SUN       │                                                INNER\n                        ├─── Sūrya      (Illuminator)                    SELF\n                        │\n                        └─── Prājāpatya (Son of Prajāpati)\n                                            │\n                                            │\n                              vyūha raśmīn  │  samūha tejaḥ\n                              (disperse)    │  (gather)\n                                            ▼\n                                  kalyāṇa-tamaṃ rūpam\n                                  (most-auspicious form)\n                                            │\n                                            ▼\n                            yo ’sāv asau puruṣaḥ so ’ham asmi\n                            ───────────────────────────────────\n                            &quot;That Person yonder — I AM He&quot;\n\n                            ←  the Īśā&#x27;s MAHĀ-VĀKYA  →</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 5","title":"Mantras 17–18: the threefold dissolution and the ascent","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<pre>                           ┌──────────────────────────┐\n                           │   the dying ātman        │\n                           │  (the integral seer of   │\n                           │       mantras 6–16)      │\n                           └────────────┬─────────────┘\n                                        │\n              ┌─────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┐\n              │                         │                         │\n              ▼                         ▼                         ▼\n        vāyuḥ →               idaṃ śarīram →             oṃ krato smara\n        anilam                bhasmāntam                 kṛtaṃ smara\n        (breath →             (body →                    (will,\n         immortal              ashes)                     remember!)\n         wind)\n              │                         │                         │\n              └─────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┘\n                                        │\n                                        ▼\n                          ┌─────────────────────────┐\n                          │  agne naya supathā      │\n                          │  rāye asmān...          │   ← mantra 18:\n                          │  yuyodhy asmaj          │     the request\n                          │  juhurāṇam enaḥ         │     to be led on\n                          └────────────┬────────────┘     the DEVAYĀNA\n                                       │                   (good path)\n                                       ▼\n                          ┌─────────────────────────┐\n                          │  bhūyiṣṭhām te          │   ← integration:\n                          │  nama-uktiṃ vidhema     │     the speech-of-\n                          └─────────────────────────┘     homage IS the\n                                                          form of so’ham</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical Steps and Observations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Īśā is uniquely a sādhana-Upaniṣad as well as a metaphysical one: every mantra has a corollary in practice. The eight sections below are organised so that they can be taken up sequentially across days of a single retreat, or revisited daily as a year-long programme."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"1. The morning Īśa-vāsya watch (mantra 1)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["On waking, before opening the eyes, mentally place over the entire field of the day the formula: ‘īśā vāsyam idaṃ sarvam.’ Each object you encounter that day — the cup, the phone, the colleague — is mentally clothed in Lord-awareness. The discipline is to neither possess nor refuse: enjoy through that-renounced.","Sivananda’s version: a five-minute pause at the threshold of every transition (room-to-room, conversation-to-task) in which the formula is silently renewed."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2. The hundred-year resolve (mantra 2)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Write down the work you would do, today, if you were certain of living a hundred years and equally certain of dying tonight. The two lists, in the Īśā’s view, should be identical: kurvann eveha karmāṇi.","Observation: in practice they diverge — the morning list is bloated with anxious self-protective work; the evening list is short and luminous. Bring the morning list to match the evening list."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"3. The ātma-han test (mantra 3)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["At the end of each day, ask: in what particular action did I behave today as though there were no Self in me? Where did I treat myself as a mere bundle of impulses? That is the day’s ātma-han, and the next day’s correction."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"4. The motion-and-rest sit (mantras 4–5)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["A daily 20-minute sitting practice: take one of the four pairs of mantra 5 (move/not-move, far/near, within/outside, swifter-than-mind/unmoving) and hold both poles together in awareness until the spatial-and-temporal categories collapse. This is not visualisation; it is the dropping of category."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"5. The not-recoiling practice (mantras 6–7)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Identify, today, the one being or class of beings from which you most recoil. Sit with the question: ‘Is this not the Self?’ Not as theory but as bodily inquiry. The verse does not promise immediate non-aversion — but it locates the structural condition of revulsion and removes it."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"6. The eight epithets meditation (mantra 8)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Take one of the eight epithets per week (śukram, akāyam, avraṇam, asnāviram, śuddham, apāpa-viddham, kavi-manīṣī-paribhūḥ-svayambhūḥ taken as a fourfold composite, vyadadhāc cha-arthān): meditate on it for one sitting, and return to the same epithet for one observation each day. The order in the verse is climactic."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"7. The two-handed sādhana (mantras 9–14)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Hold daily life in two columns: ‘karma I performed’ (avidyā / sambhūti) and ‘knowing I sat in’ (vidyā / asambhūti). Each evening, examine: did either column run dry today? The Īśā’s diagnosis is unambiguous — a dry knowing column produces moral darkness; a dry karma column produces a worse, subtler one.","The synthesis (mantras 11 and 14) is not a third practice but the holding of both columns in conscious tension as a single life."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"8. The sunrise / sunset practice (mantras 15–16)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["At first or last light — eyes barely open, never gazing directly at the sun — recite mantra 15 once and mantra 16 once. Allow the rays to ‘gather.’ End by sitting in silence with ‘so’ham asmi’ until the meditation closes itself. Sivananda’s instruction: do not try to extend; let the practice end when it ends."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"9. The antya-smaraṇa rehearsal (mantras 17–18)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Once a week, recite mantras 17 and 18 lying down at the end of the day, as if it were the last evening. Notice (i) which actions of the day the will (krato) presents for remembrance, (ii) which actions are crooked (juhurāṇam eno) and require yuyodhi, and (iii) what the most-abundant speech-of-homage (bhūyiṣṭhām nama-uktim) would actually consist of. The practice is the structural rehearsal of integral dying that the Upaniṣad ends with."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Cross-references in the Upaniṣadic Corpus","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Īśā is short but echo-rich. The table below collects every significant intertextual parallel — both verses other Upaniṣads draw from the Īśā and verses the Īśā itself echoes from older sources (Ṛgveda, Atharvaveda, Brāhmaṇas).","<table><tr><td>Īśā mantra</td><td>Parallel passage</td><td>Relationship</td></tr><tr><td>Śānti-pāṭha (pūrṇam adaḥ…)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka Upaniṣad 5.1.1</td><td>Both Upaniṣads share this śānti as the canonical Śukla-Yajurveda opening; the BĀU expands it doctrinally.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 1 (īśā vāsyam idaṃ sarvam)</td><td>Atharvaveda 10.8 (skambha-sūkta); Bhagavad-Gītā 9.4 (mayā tatam idaṃ sarvam)</td><td>AV provides the cosmos-as-garment image; the Gītā quotes the formula almost verbatim.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 2 (kurvann eveha karmāṇi)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka Upaniṣad 4.4.22; Bhagavad-Gītā 3.20 (loka-saṃgraham evāpi sampaśyan)</td><td>The BĀU contrast-pole; the Gītā elaborates the karma-yoga reading.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 3 (asuryā nāma te lokā)</td><td>Kaṭha Upaniṣad 1.2.6 (na sāmparāyaḥ pratibhāti bālam); Maitrāyaṇī Upaniṣad 4.2</td><td>Kaṭha echoes the doctrine of the Self-denying perishing into darkness.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 4 (anejad ekaṃ manaso javīyaḥ)</td><td>Kaṭha Upaniṣad 1.2.21 (āsīno dūraṃ vrajati); Muṇḍaka 2.1.2; Kena 1.4</td><td>Three parallel formulations of the moving-yet-unmoving Ātman.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 5 (tad ejati tan naijati)</td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 13.15–16 (sarvendriya-guṇa-ābhāsam …)</td><td>The Gītā develops the tetra-paradox into a sustained passage.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 6 (yastu sarvāṇi bhūtāny ātmany evānupaśyati)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 4.4.23; Bhagavad-Gītā 6.29 (sarva-bhūta-stham ātmānam)</td><td>BĀU gives the prose form; the Gītā the metrical.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 7 (yasmin sarvāṇi bhūtāni)</td><td>Chāndogya 7.1.3 (Nārada’s sorrow); Bhagavad-Gītā 2.11 (na śocanti)</td><td>The Gītā opens with this Upaniṣadic doctrine of the no-grief of the knower.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 8 (sa paryagāc chukram akāyam)</td><td>Muṇḍaka 1.1.6; Praśna 6.2</td><td>Muṇḍaka has the eight-epithet parallel; Praśna echoes the apāpa-viddham.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantras 9 &amp; 12 (andhaṃ tamaḥ praviśanti)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 4.4.10 (nearly verbatim); Kaṭha 1.2.5</td><td>The Īśā has drawn directly from BĀU 4.4.10; the verse-pair structure is the Īśā’s own innovation.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 11 (vidyāṃ cāvidyāṃ ca)</td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 5.4–5 (sāṅkhya-yogau pṛthag bālāḥ)</td><td>The Gītā re-frames the karma-jñāna integration in different vocabulary.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantras 12–14 (sambhūti / asambhūti)</td><td>Māṇḍūkya 7 (śāntaṃ śivam advaitaṃ); Taittirīya 2.7 (asad vā idam agra āsīt)</td><td>Taittirīya is the locus classicus for the asat-as-source-of-sat doctrine that the Īśā here problematises.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 15 (hiraṇmayena pātreṇa)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 5.15.1 (the same mantra appears there word-for-word as a sandhya-mantra); Chāndogya 1.6.6</td><td>The most striking single overlap in the Upaniṣadic corpus — the same five mantras (Īśā 15–18) appear in BĀU 5.15.1–4.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 16 (so ’ham asmi)</td><td>Chāndogya 6.8.7 (tat tvam asi); Māṇḍūkya 2 (ayam ātmā brahma); Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 1.4.10 (ahaṃ brahmāsmi)</td><td>All four mahā-vākyas of the Vedāntic tradition. The Īśā’s ‘so’ham asmi’ is the first-person reflex of the Chāndogya’s second-person ‘tat tvam asi.’</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 17 (vāyur anilam amṛtam)</td><td>Atharvaveda 18.2 (funeral hymns); Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 5.15.2</td><td>The funeral-mantra origin is clearest in the AV parallels.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 18 (agne naya supathā rāye)</td><td>Ṛgveda 1.189.1 (verbatim); Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 5.15.4</td><td>The unique case of a Ṛgvedic verse incorporated unchanged into the principal-Upaniṣad corpus.</td></tr></table>","The most consequential cross-reference: mantras 15–18 of the Īśā appear, in order, as Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 5.15.1–4. The two principal Upaniṣads of the Śukla-Yajurveda share a single closing block of four mantras. Whether the Īśā has drawn from the BĀU or the BĀU has drawn from a common older source is a long-standing scholarly question; Olivelle inclines to the latter."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Sanskrit text","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Vājasaneyī-Saṃhitā (Śukla-Yajurveda), Mādhyandina recension, chapter 40 — sanskritdocuments.org, ITRANS encoding (file: IshaavaasyaUpanishad.itx).","Kāṇva recension — same source, cross-reference file (variants noted in §I.2).","Critical text in Olivelle, Patrick (1998), The Early Upaniṣads: Annotated Text and Translation, Oxford University Press."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"The five commentaries","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Gambhirananda, Swami (tr.) (1957/repr.), Eight Upaniṣads with the Commentary of Śaṅkarācārya, Vol. 1, Advaita Ashrama, Kolkata — contains Śaṅkara’s Īśāvāsya-bhāṣya in English.","Sivananda, Swami (1937, repr. 1972), The Principal Upanishads, Divine Life Society, Rishikesh — Īśāvāsya translation with running commentary; supplemented by Sivananda, Īśāvāsya: Its Esoteric Meaning (DLS lecture transcripts).","Krishnananda, Swami (1971), The Philosophy of the Īśāvāsya Upaniṣad, Divine Life Society, Rishikesh; supplemented by the recorded Rishikesh lectures (DLS audio archive).","Deussen, Paul (1897), Sechzig Upaniṣad’s des Veda aus dem Sanskrit übersetzt, F. A. Brockhaus, Leipzig; English translation: Sixty Upaniṣads of the Veda, tr. V. M. Bedekar and G. B. Palsule, Motilal Banarsidass, Delhi, 1980.","Aurobindo, Sri (1924, rev. 1965), Īsha Upanishad, Sri Aurobindo Ashram, Pondicherry; in Volume 17 of the Complete Works of Sri Aurobindo (CWSA)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Secondary scholarship","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Hume, R. E. (1921, 2nd ed. 1931), The Thirteen Principal Upanishads, Oxford University Press.","Radhakrishnan, S. (1953), The Principal Upaniṣads, George Allen &amp; Unwin, London — extensive notes on the Īśā.","Ranade, R. D. (1926), A Constructive Survey of Upaniṣadic Philosophy, Bharatiya Vidya Bhavan, repr. 1968.","Cohen, Signe (ed.) (2018), The Upanishads: A Complete Guide, Routledge — Olivelle’s chapter on the Īśā.","Roebuck, Valerie J. (2003), The Upaniṣads, Penguin Classics — accessible literary translation with notes."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Sister-Upaniṣads of the Śukla-Yajurveda","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka Upaniṣad — the only other principal Upaniṣad of the Śukla-Yajurveda; mantras 15–18 of the Īśā appear in BĀU 5.15.1–4.","Subāla Upaniṣad — a minor Upaniṣad of the Śukla-Yajurveda; develops Īśā mantra 1’s īśā-vāsya theme."],"kind":"note"}]}]};
